4.3.3 Privacy and RBAC Middleware

The Privacy and RBAC Middleware provides a secure enforcement layer that governs access to Validium-based off-chain state and orchestrates blockchain transaction execution under strict role-based policies.

Off-chain Privacy and Validium Control

In this architecture, sensitive data and transaction states are stored off-chain within Validium private storage, ensuring that only zero-knowledge proofs are posted to Layer 1. This prevents exposure of confidential business information while guaranteeing verifiability through cryptographic proofs.
	•	On-chain: only validity proofs and hashes are committed.
	•	Off-chain: transaction intents, balances, and settlement details remain private, queryable only by authorized participants.
	•	Logs and calldata: enriched human-readable details are made available in event logs, minimizing on-chain storage costs and preserving confidentiality.

RBAC Enforcement

Access to Validium RPC endpoints and private storage is gated by Role-Based Access Control (RBAC). Each request is validated against policies that define:
	•	Who (user identity, API key, JWT claims).
	•	What (contract, function selector, resource scope).
	•	How (permissions, quotas, transaction type, nonce safety).

This ensures that only authorized entities can prepare and submit blockchain transactions or query confidential off-chain state.

Prototype vs Production
	•	Prototype Phase: RBAC policies are mocked within the middleware to validate functional flows without requiring full identity infrastructure. This enables rapid iteration and integration testing.
	•	Production Phase: RBAC is integrated with an Identity Provider (IdP) for fine-grained authorization and an API Gateway for authentication. External users authenticate via credentials; JWT tokens are issued and validated against RBAC policies before any transaction is processed.

Delegated Transaction Model

The Backend API does not broadcast blockchain transactions directly. Instead, it delegates transaction preparation and submission to the RBAC Middleware:
	1.	Prepare: Business logic in the backoffice assembles the transaction intent (using EIP-712 typed structured data).
	2.	Authorize: Middleware validates intent against RBAC rules, allowlists, and quotas.
	3.	Sign/Relay: If authorized, the Middleware signs the transaction (via KMS/HSM) or relays it on behalf of the user.
	4.	Execute: Settlement occurs on-chain, with confidentiality preserved off-chain.
EIP-1159 (Ethereum Improvement Proposal 1159)

Also known as: London Hard Fork / Fee Market Change / “Base Fee” model
	•	Introduced in August 2021 with the London upgrade.
	•	Changed how transaction fees (gas fees) are calculated on Ethereum.

Before (old model):
	•	Users set a gas price (bid).
	•	Miners picked transactions with the highest bids → fee auction.
	•	Problem: fees were volatile, spikes during congestion.

After (EIP-1159):
	•	Transactions now include:
	•	Base Fee: algorithmically set by the protocol, changes block to block depending on network demand.
	•	Priority Fee (tip): optional extra you pay to incentivize miners/validators.
	•	Max Fee: maximum total you are willing to pay.

Benefits:
	•	More predictable gas prices.
	•	Better UX for wallets/dapps (no guessing fees).
	•	Base fee is burned → reduces ETH supply (deflationary pressure).

